1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,193
A site asks the same questions again and again:

2
00:00:04,193 --> 00:00:07,989
the posts of a blog, a search, a count from the database.

3
00:00:07,989 --> 00:00:11,182
The Queries module saves each one under a name.

4
00:00:11,182 --> 00:00:15,640
You create a query, run it, deploy it, and add a source of your own.

5
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:27,207
Without saved queries, the same SQL or search text is copied into templates, scripts and controllers.

6
00:00:27,207 --> 00:00:31,830
When the question changes, every copy has to be found and edited.

7
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:37,137
The Queries module keeps each question in one place, under a name.

8
00:00:37,137 --> 00:00:41,314
A query has a Name, a Source, an optional Schema, and a flag

9
00:00:41,314 --> 00:00:44,415
that says whether it returns content items.

10
00:00:44,415 --> 00:00:47,200
The source decides how the query runs.

11
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:53,345
Queries Core Services holds the query manager, the recipe step and the Liquid

12
00:00:53,345 --> 00:00:57,440
filter, and is enabled by the features that depend on it.

13
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:00,820
Queries adds the screens, permissions and API.

14
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:04,850
SQL Queries, Lucene and Elasticsearch each add a source.

15
00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:12,997
Let's open Search, Queries, All Queries in the admin menu.

16
00:01:12,997 --> 00:01:18,214
A site created with the Blog recipe has one saved query, RecentBlogPosts, which

17
00:01:18,214 --> 00:01:20,300
the Blog theme runs by name.

18
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:24,101
Each row shows the name and a badge for the source.

19
00:01:24,101 --> 00:01:28,262
SQL Queries adds a Run button to its queries, and every query has

20
00:01:28,262 --> 00:01:30,020
Edit and Delete buttons.

21
00:01:30,472 --> 00:01:33,347
Add Query opens Available Query Types.

22
00:01:33,347 --> 00:01:36,811
Each source feature adds a card to this dialog.

23
00:01:36,811 --> 00:01:41,190
On this site, SQL is the only one, because SQL Queries is the

24
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:43,412
only source feature enabled.

25
00:01:47,634 --> 00:01:52,293
To create a query, choose Add Query, then Add on the card of its source.

26
00:01:52,293 --> 00:01:56,474
The page names the source in its title, and the form starts with

27
00:01:56,474 --> 00:01:58,624
the fields every query shares.

28
00:01:59,078 --> 00:02:01,912
Name is the technical name every caller uses.

29
00:02:01,912 --> 00:02:06,136
It takes letters and digits, starts with a letter, and must be unique.

30
00:02:06,136 --> 00:02:08,748
Let's call this query PublishedBlogPosts.

31
00:02:09,204 --> 00:02:10,716
Schema is optional.

32
00:02:10,716 --> 00:02:13,983
It tells the GraphQL API what the query returns.

33
00:02:13,983 --> 00:02:16,948
The rest of the form comes from the source:

34
00:02:16,948 --> 00:02:22,514
for SQL, Return Documents loads the content items whose document identifiers the query

35
00:02:22,514 --> 00:02:24,874
selects, and Query holds the SQL.

36
00:02:25,357 --> 00:02:30,300
Let's check Return Documents, and enter a query that selects the document identifiers

37
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:32,147
of the published blog posts.

38
00:02:32,625 --> 00:02:36,362
Save adds the query to the list, and a message confirms it.

39
00:02:36,362 --> 00:02:40,445
Its row has the same source badge and buttons as RecentBlogPosts.

40
00:02:40,895 --> 00:02:44,381
As the list grows, the search box filters it by name.

41
00:02:44,381 --> 00:02:48,575
Let's search for Published, which leaves only PublishedBlogPosts.

42
00:02:52,775 --> 00:02:55,013
A saved query runs by its name.

43
00:02:55,013 --> 00:03:00,032
Liquid reads it from the Queries property and applies the query filter, while

44
00:03:00,032 --> 00:03:03,720
Razor, scripts and the API call the same query manager.

45
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:06,865
A separate video covers templates and the API.

46
00:03:07,615 --> 00:03:10,742
The API checks a permission for each query:

47
00:03:10,742 --> 00:03:15,082
Execute Api - All queries, or one named for the query, such as

48
00:03:15,082 --> 00:03:17,379
Execute Api - RecentBlogPosts.

49
00:03:17,379 --> 00:03:19,613
Manage queries includes both.

50
00:03:19,613 --> 00:03:22,995
A caller without the permission gets Not Found.

51
00:03:27,195 --> 00:03:31,060
The Queries recipe step saves queries when a recipe runs.

52
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:35,846
Each entry gives a Source and a Name, plus the properties of its source.

53
00:03:35,846 --> 00:03:38,545
A query with the same Name is updated.

54
00:03:39,295 --> 00:03:44,682
In a deployment plan, the All Queries step exports every query as a Queries step.

55
00:03:44,682 --> 00:03:49,946
The Queried Content Items step exports the content items one query returns, and

56
00:03:49,946 --> 00:03:53,475
refuses a query that does not return content items.

57
00:03:57,675 --> 00:04:01,382
To add a source of your own, implement IQuerySource.

58
00:04:01,382 --> 00:04:08,667
Its Name identifies the source, and ExecuteQueryAsync takes a Query and parameters, and returns the results.

59
00:04:08,667 --> 00:04:13,268
Values specific to the source, such as the SQL text, are read from

60
00:04:13,268 --> 00:04:15,505
metadata stored on the query.

61
00:04:16,255 --> 00:04:19,535
Register the source with AddQuerySource and its name.

62
00:04:19,535 --> 00:04:23,260
The query manager finds a source by that name, so a query can

63
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:26,985
be created and run only when its Source is a registered name.

64
00:04:27,735 --> 00:04:31,900
A DisplayDriver for Query builds the source's part of the form, such as

65
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:34,117
Return Documents and Query for SQL.

66
00:04:34,117 --> 00:04:38,228
It returns nothing unless the query's Source is its own, and on update

67
00:04:38,228 --> 00:04:41,365
it stores the source's values on the query with Put.

68
00:04:42,115 --> 00:04:46,840
A Link view named after the source draws its card in the Add Query dialog.

69
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:51,387
SQL, Lucene and Elasticsearch each have one, and its Add link opens the

70
00:04:51,387 --> 00:04:53,395
create page for that source.

71
00:04:54,145 --> 00:04:58,599
A source reads its own properties from recipe data in an IQueryHandler.

72
00:04:58,599 --> 00:05:03,170
The SQL source checks the query's Source, then copies the Template when a

73
00:05:03,170 --> 00:05:05,715
recipe initializes or updates a query.

74
00:05:07,715 --> 00:05:09,638
That is the Queries module:

75
00:05:09,638 --> 00:05:14,009
a named query, kept in one place, run from templates, scripts and the

76
00:05:14,009 --> 00:05:18,730
API, moved with recipes and deployments, and extended with your own source.

77
00:05:18,730 --> 00:05:20,245
Thanks for watching.

